WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.042
"Can Goitrogens in Soy and Cruciferous Vegetables
Interfere with Thyroid Function?"

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.400
A healthy lifestyle
and preventable death?

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.000
What are the six
lifestyle factors

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.680
that collectively appeared
to cut the risk

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.320
of dying prematurely in half?

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.480
Not smoking,
not drinking heavily,

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:19.680
walking an hour a day,

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.600
sleeping around
seven hours a night,

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:22.800
not being overweight,

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.840
and the sixth is the one
dietary quality indicator

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.400
they used,
eating your greens.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.240
Most people don't eat enough,

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.040
but is it possible
to eat too many?

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.360
I've talked about the risks
of getting too many oxalates

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:36.880
by eating too many
of certain greens,

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.440
like spinach and Swiss chard,

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.400
but what about
low-oxalate greens

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.080
like kale or collards?

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.840
This cabbage family
of vegetables

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.480
has natural
goitrogenic compounds

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.200
that can interfere
with thyroid function.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.120
They can compete with our
thyroid's uptake of iodine.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.440
But the answer is not to avoid
these super healthy foods,

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.720
but, rather, to get
enough iodine in our diet,

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.400
as I've talked about before.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.640
There is, however,
an extreme threshold

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.160
we don't want to cross.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:05.840
How much is too much?

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:07.440
From the New England
Journal of Medicine:

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.480
Coma Induced by
Ingestion of Raw Bok Choy.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.640
A woman had been eating
about three pounds (1.30 kg)

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.000
a day, every day,

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.520
which means 15 cups (~450g)
a day is too much.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:20.400
In the two months before

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.640
she was admitted
to the intensive care unit

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.320
in respiratory failure

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.200
with a diagnosis
of severe hypothyroidism,

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.680
she had consumed at least
1,000 cups of raw bok choy.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.280
The reason raw was specified

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:34.960
is that the enzyme
that creates

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:36.720
these iodine-blocking compounds

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.200
when we bite into them
or chop them up

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.920
is rapidly deactivated
by cooking,

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:43.200
so we don't have
to worry as much

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.000
about eating
too many cooked crucifers.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.760
There are also
goitrogenic compounds

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:48.880
in soybeans,

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.560
though there is
little evidence that

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:51.600
this presents a problem

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.520
for people with
normal thyroid function

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.200
who are getting enough iodine.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.600
Now, if you have hypothyroidism

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.560
and are taking
thyroid hormone replacement,

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.440
soy may impair
the absorption of the drug.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:05.280
But that is not unique to soy,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.120
that's why
hypothyroidism patients

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:08.720
are told to take
their Synthroid

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:09.520
before breakfast,

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.560
or at least four hours
after a meal.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.200
There have been over a dozen
randomized, controlled trials

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.760
on the effect of soy
on thyroid function.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.040
Researchers found that soy

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.080
has no effect
on thyroid hormone levels,

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.760
but there was
a 10 percent elevation in TSH.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:26.880
That may not mean anything

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.520
for people
with healthy thyroid glands,

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.240
but if your thyroid function
is already suboptimal

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.720
that could potentially
tip you over the edge.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.360
About 1 in every 23 Americans

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.640
has what's called
subclinical hypothyroidism.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.960
This is when our thyroid gland
continues to produce

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.440
normal levels
of thyroid hormones

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.640
but seems to have a more
difficult time than usual,

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:52.240
as evidenced by higher levels
of thyroid stimulating hormone.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.400
Meaning our body
has to stimulate our thyroid

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.360
more than usual to produce
normal levels of hormones,

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.840
suggesting it may
be starting to fail.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.520
Okay, so, 60 patients
with subclinical hypothyroidism

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.320
were randomized to eight weeks
of either the equivalent

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:10.080
of about two servings
of soy foods a day or nothing.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.240
And six patients developed
overt hypothyroidism

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.840
while on the soy,

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.720
which is three times the rate
that would have been expected.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:22.720
But the soy group
had a bunch of benefits,

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.840
like less insulin resistance,
lower inflammation,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.640
and better blood pressures.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.360
However, the thyroid findings
were so concerning

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.160
that the same research group
ran the study again,

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.680
but this time with
four times the dose of soy

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.240
and this time
there was no increase

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.720
in overt hypothyroidism.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:42.720
What are we to make of all this?

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.800
Well, soy industry consultants

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.800
concluded that
these follow-up results

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.280
call into question
the initial findings.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.200
I mean, if four times
the amount of soy

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.320
appeared to have no effect

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.720
then maybe the earlier
findings were a fluke.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.720
But the study populations
were different.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:59.040
In the later study,
the patients started out

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:02.320
with TSH levels that were
only barely elevated.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.840
Normal levels go up
to 4.5 or 5,

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.080
so they were just over the line,

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:09.520
whereas in the earlier studies

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:11.600
they started out closer to 8,

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.280
meaning the patients
were further along

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.760
and therefore
potentially more sensitive.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:18.800
If you follow people
with subclinical hypothyroidism

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:19.840
over time,

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:23.120
most patients who start out
with TSH levels over six

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.640
end up with overt hypothyroidism
within a decade,

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.320
but none of those
who started out

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.520
with levels between four and six
suffered the same fate.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.840
So, if your TSH
is really elevated

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.480
it might be reasonable
to avoid soy,

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.008
but we'd need more research
to know for sure.

